# ===========================================================================
# CECODES - Huella de Carbono
#
# Local development:  copy to `.env.local`
# Docker deployment:  copy to `.env`      (docker-compose.yml reads `.env`)
#
# Everything below is grouped by whether you actually have to set it.
#
# A note on the quotes below, learned the hard way. `docker compose` strips surrounding quotes
# from an env file, so the values here work as written. `docker run --env-file` does NOT: it
# passes "postgresql://..." through with the quote characters still in the string, and the
# connection then fails with nothing in the message to suggest why. If you ever run a container
# by hand rather than through compose, drop the quotes.
# ===========================================================================


# ===========================================================================
# REQUIRED - the app refuses to start without these
#
# src/instrumentation.ts validates the first three at boot, and
# scripts/init-db.ts validates all six before touching the database. A
# missing one fails immediately with the variable named, rather than a 500
# on the first request.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Supabase project API (dashboard -> Project Settings -> API) ---
#
# NOTE: the two NEXT_PUBLIC_ values are baked into the image at BUILD time,
# because Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* during compilation. Change either and
# you must rebuild: `docker compose up -d --build`. One image therefore
# belongs to one Supabase project.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="<anon-public-key>"

# Server-only key with full database rights. NEVER expose it to the client,
# never commit it, never paste it into a log or an issue.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="<service-role-key>"

# --- Database (dashboard -> Project Settings -> Database) ---
#
# Two URLs for one database, deliberately. The app runs many short-lived
# serverless-style queries and uses the POOLED port; Prisma migrations need a
# session-mode connection a pooler cannot give, so they use the DIRECT port.

# Pooled (Supavisor, 6543) - used by the app at runtime, src/lib/prisma.ts
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<db-password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"

# Direct (5432) - used by prisma migrate, prisma.config.ts
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<db-password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# --- The first admin, created idempotently by prisma/seed.ts ---
#
# This is the ONLY way into a fresh system: self-serve registration is off
# (src/lib/feature-flags.ts, FEATURE_SELF_ONBOARDING = false). If these are
# missing, initialization now FAILS rather than quietly finishing with no
# account. Re-running the seed keeps the password in sync with this file.
ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@example.com"
ADMIN_PASSWORD="<strong-password-min-12-chars>"


# ===========================================================================
# OPTIONAL - sensible defaults, set only if you need them
# ===========================================================================

# Public hostname, used only by the optional TLS proxy:
#   docker compose --profile edge up -d
# DNS must already point at this server. Unset means http://localhost.
# DOMAIN="huella.example.org"

# Port the app listens on inside its container. Rarely worth changing; the
# published binding lives in docker-compose.yml.
# PORT="3000"

# Public origin used to build the links in password-reset and confirmation
# emails. It is pinned in configuration because the alternative is reading the
# request's Host header, which an attacker controls: a crafted request would
# have Supabase mail a real user a real token pointing at another site.
#
# Usually you do not need this. DOMAIN above already answers the question, and
# SITE_URL is only for a deployment whose public address differs from it. With
# neither set, emailed links are built in development from the request host and
# in production not at all, so a reset mail fails visibly instead of pointing
# somewhere unintended.
#
# It must be a full URL including the scheme, not a bare hostname like DOMAIN
# above. The app now checks that at boot and refuses to start on anything else.
# Until it did, a hostname here was silently discarded: the override you set on
# purpose was ignored, and the links fell back to DOMAIN, or to nowhere when
# DOMAIN was unset too.
# SITE_URL="https://huella.example.org"

# Password-reset email, sent through Resend (https://resend.com).
#
# Both or neither, and the app refuses to start on one without the other: a key
# with no From address cannot address a message, and a From address with no key
# cannot send one. Half a configuration is never something you meant, and it is
# invisible otherwise - the app boots, every page works, and password reset is
# quietly dead. Leave them BOTH unset and the app runs normally with one feature
# missing - "I forgot my password" is refused up front instead of accepting the
# request and dropping the mail, and an admin sets the member's password by hand
# as before.
#
# MAIL_FROM is a From header, so either a bare address or "Name <address>".
# The domain has to be one you verified with Resend.
# RESEND_API_KEY="<resend-api-key>"
# MAIL_FROM="CECODES <no-reply@huella.example.org>"

# Where a password is checked, while this app moves off Supabase Auth onto
# hashes kept in its own database:
#
#   supabase  Supabase decides every sign-in. This is today's behaviour, and
#             leaving the variable unset is how you keep it. Nothing changes.
#   shadow    Supabase still decides, but the local hash is checked alongside
#             it and any disagreement is logged. Nobody is locked out by a bad
#             hash; the log is how the backfill earns trust before the cutover.
#   local     Self-hosted only. Supabase is never asked for a password.
#
# Any other value and the app refuses to start, naming the variable.
# AUTH_PROVIDER="supabase"


# ===========================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT ONLY - must never be set on a deployed server
# ===========================================================================

# Demo companies and sample activity data (prisma/seed-demo.ts, run with
# `bun run db:seed:demo`).
#
# There is ONE shared Supabase database, so this variable is the only thing
# standing between that script and real client data. scripts/init-db.ts
# refuses to run at all when it is "true", so an automated deployment cannot
# create demo tenants by accident.
DEMO_SEED_ALLOWED="false"
DEMO_PASSWORD="<demo-password>"

# Skips admin creation during `bun run db:seed`. Only for working against a
# database whose admin already exists. Never set this in a deployment: it
# turns the fail-loud check above back into a silent skip.
# SEED_SKIP_ADMIN="false"
